 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_n23tkV4bCqrNVV52",
   "name": "Volumes of revolution around the x-axis",
   "definition": "",
   "video_id": "856378203"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Volumes of revolution around the x-axis"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "What is a volume of revolution around the x-axis?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A **solid** of **revolution** is formed when an **area** bounded by a function $y=f(x)$ (and other boundary equations) is rotated 360° around the *x*-axis\n- A **volume** of **revolution** is the volume of this solid formed"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/06/2dk4S6Oy_6-2-4-cie-fig1-vol-of-rev.png",
   "file": "assets/d2404d7ea20a-2dk4s6oy-6-2-4-cie-fig1-vol-of-rev.png",
   "alt": "2dk4S6Oy_6-2-4-cie-fig1-vol-of-rev",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***Example of a solid of revolution that is formed by rotating the area bounded by the function ***$y=f(x)$ ***, the lines ***$x=a$ *** and  ***$x=b$*** and the ***$x$***-axis ***$360^{\\circ}$*** about the ***$x$***-axis***"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "How do I find the volume of revolution around the x-axis?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- To find the **volume** of **revolution** created when the area bounded by the function $y=f(x)$, the lines $x=a$ and $x=b$, and the *x*-axis is rotated 360° about the *x*-axis use the formula"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$V = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} y^{2} d x$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The formula may look complicated or confusing at first due to the *y* and d*x*\n\n  - remember that *y* is a function of *x*\n  - once the expression for *y* is substituted in, everything will be in terms of *x*\n- π is a constant so you may see this written either inside or outside the integral\n- This is **not** given in the formulae booklet\n\n  - The formulae booklet does list the volume formulae for some common 3D solids – it may be possible to use these depending on what information about the solid is available"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "How do I solve problems involving volumes of revolution around the x-axis?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Visualising** the solid created is helpful\n\n  - Try sketching some functions and their solids of revolution to help\n- **STEP 1** Square *y*\n\n  - Do this first without worrying about π or the integration and limits\n- **STEP 2** Identify the limits *a* and *b* (which could come from a graph)\n- **STEP 3** Use the formula by evaluating the integral and multiplying by π\n\n  - The answer may be required in **exact form **(leave in terms of π)\n  \n    - If not, round to **three significant figures** (unless told otherwise)\n- Trickier questions may give you the volume and ask for the value of an unknown constant elsewhere in the problem"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "![6-2-4-cie-fig2-we-solution](assets/e724651c5b63-6-2-4-cie-fig2-we-solution.png)"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- To help remember the formula note that it is only $y^{2}$ - volume is 3D so you may have expected a cubic expression\n\n  - If rotating a **single** **point** around the *x*-axis a **circle** of radius would be formed\n  \n    - The **area** of that circle would then be $πy^{2}$\n    - Integration then adds up the areas of all circles between *a* and *b* creating the third dimension and **volume**\n    (In 2D, integration creates area by adding up lots of 1D lines)"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_VrW7TGsVKVSz59w3",
   "name": "Volumes of revolution around the y-axis",
   "definition": "",
   "video_id": "857042403"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Volumes of revolution around the y-axis"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "What is a volume of revolution around the y-axis?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A **solid** of **revolution** is formed when an **area** bounded by a function $y=f(x)$ (and other boundary equations) is rotated 360° around the *y*-axis\n- A **volume** of **revolution** is the volume of this solid formed"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/06/6-2-4-cie-fig3-vol-of-rev-y-axis.png",
   "file": "assets/265af3ce15fe-6-2-4-cie-fig3-vol-of-rev-y-axis.png",
   "alt": "6-2-4-cie-fig3-vol-of-rev-y-axis",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***Example of a solid of revolution that is formed by rotating the area bounded by the function ***$y=f(x)$ ***, the lines ***$y=c$*** and  ***$y=d$***and the ***$x$***-axis ***$360^{\\circ}$*** about the ***$y$***-axis***"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "How do I find the volume of revolution around the y-axis?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- To find the **volume** of **revolution** created when the area bounded by the function $y=f(x)$, the lines $y=c$ and $y=d$, and the *y*-axis is rotated 360° about the *y*-axis use the formula"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$V = \\pi \\int_{c}^{d} x^{2} d y$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Note that although the function may be given in the form $y=f(x)$ it will first need rewriting in the form $x=g(y)$\n- This is **not** given in the formulae booklet"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "How do I solve problems involving volumes of revolution around the y-axis?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Visualising** the solid created is helpful\n\n  - Try sketching some functions and their solids of revolution to help"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **STEP 1** Rearrange $y=f(x)$ into the form $x=g(y)$ (if necessary)\n\n  - This is finding the **inverse** **function **$f^{-1}(x)$\n- **STEP 2** Square *x*\n\n  - Do this first without worrying about π or the integration and limits\n- **STEP 3** Identify the limits *c* and *d* (which could come from a graph)\n- **STEP 4** Use the formula by evaluating the integral and multiplying by π\n\n  - The answer may be required in **exact form **(leave in terms of π)\n  \n    - If not, round to **three significant figures** (unless told otherwise)"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Trickier questions may give you the volume and ask for the value of an unknown constant elsewhere in the problem"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "![6-2-4-cie-fig4-we-solution-part-1](assets/435e3e3da6eb-6-2-4-cie-fig4-we-solution-part-1.png)\n\n![6-2-4-cie-fig4-we-solution-part-2](assets/390031734ee1-6-2-4-cie-fig4-we-solution-part-2.png)"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- Double check questions to ensure you are clear about which axis the rotation is around\n- Separating the rearranging of $y=f(x)$into $x=g(y)$ and the squaring of *x* is important for maintaining accuracy\n\n  - In some cases it can seem as though *x* has been squared twice\n  \n    - in the worked example above, *x* has been squared twice\n    - but it needed to be – once as part of the rearranging, once as part of the volume formula"
  }
 ]
